-- Generated by `drizzle-kit generate` (ADR-0008), with the assertion above the
-- constraint written by hand (ADR-0073 §8, `D20.5`).
--
-- The invariant everything downstream leans on — **every `active` user has a
-- proven address** — stops being a rule three code paths remember and becomes
-- a rule Postgres refuses to break. `packages/db/src/schema/users.ts` states
-- what it covers and what it deliberately does not; the review finding it
-- closes is `DB-04`.
--
-- **The `DO` block is the reason this file is not just the one `ALTER`.**
-- `ADD CONSTRAINT … CHECK` validates the existing rows, so on a database that
-- holds a violating row it fails on its own — with Postgres's own message,
-- which names the constraint and the table and not one offending account. That
-- message sends an operator to the schema; this one sends them to the rows. It
-- runs in the same transaction as the `ALTER` (Drizzle wraps a migration's
-- statements in one), so a failure here leaves the database exactly as it was
-- and the release gate stops before the API is replaced.
--
-- It can only fire on a database that predates `D18.3`, since which no path
-- creates such a row (`0014_mail_flows.sql` backfilled the stamp onto the
-- `active` rows that existed then). A fork whose own code took a fourth path
-- is the case this is written for, and the repair is theirs to choose: stamp
-- the address, or move the account to `pending_verification` and let the
-- verification link stamp it. Deliberately **not** a silent backfill — writing
-- `email_verified_at = now()` for a row nobody proved would make the invariant
-- true and the claim it stands for false, which is the one outcome worse than
-- a failed migration.
--
-- **This release takes a brief `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock on `users`**
-- (`D20.5`'s `security-reviewer`), and a fork with a large table should know
-- it before a deploy rather than during one. `ADD CONSTRAINT … CHECK`
-- validates every existing row, so the table is scanned under that lock — a
-- second time, the `DO` block above having already scanned it, no index
-- serving either predicate — while the **old** API is still serving, because
-- `migrate` is the release gate. Every login and every session-backed read of
-- an account waits for it. `NOT VALID` plus a later `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`
-- is the standard way out and does **not** help inside one migration, which
-- is what Drizzle wraps these statements in; it would take a second migration
-- file, and whether this repository's migrations may hold an exclusive lock
-- during a release is a decision ADR-0008 does not make. It belongs with the
-- concurrent-index lane the review routed to D25 (`DB-01`), not here.
DO $$
DECLARE
  offending bigint;
  sample text;
BEGIN
  SELECT count(*), min(id::text) INTO offending, sample
  FROM users
  WHERE status = 'active' AND email_verified_at IS NULL;

  IF offending > 0 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION
      'users_active_email_verified_check: % active user(s) have no email_verified_at (for example %). Stamp the address or move the account to pending_verification, then re-run this migration.',
      offending, sample
      USING ERRCODE = 'check_violation';
  END IF;
END $$;
--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "users" ADD CONSTRAINT "users_active_email_verified_check" CHECK ("users"."status" <> 'active' or "users"."email_verified_at" is not null);
